1
00:00:00,411 --> 00:00:10,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, the sutta for today, it's Saṃyutta Nikāya 35, 246.

2
00:00:13,714 --> 00:00:15,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably most people would have read it.

3
00:00:15,715 --> 00:00:17,615
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a famous dissimilar of the lute.

4
00:00:18,516 --> 00:00:27,938
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's really the things that are mentioned in the suttas before the dissimilar of the lute that are kind of more interesting, well, certainly more relevant for the practice.

5
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:49,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whatever bhikkhu or a bhikkhuni, regarding forms cognizable by the eye, should there arise desire, or greed, or hate, or delusion, or even resistance, patiga, of their cetasau, which is of their citta, from that they should reign in the citta.

6
00:00:50,699 --> 00:01:03,899
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I left cetas and citta in Pali because... just to kind of indicate things that are pertaining to citta and that are kind of cittas doing, to put it that way.

7
00:01:05,647 --> 00:01:12,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Because with the word mind, mind this, mind that, mano-mind, my mind, citta-mind, it kind of gets, it gets a bit blurred.

8
00:01:13,859 --> 00:01:22,571
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, same, it's like with the word resistance, patīgā, because, like, if you look at the common translations, they just say aversion.

9
00:01:23,954 --> 00:01:26,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, if there is even aversion in my mind.

10
00:01:26,795 --> 00:01:32,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, it kind of becomes the whole meaning of this very obviously short, like, what, three lines.

11
00:01:33,736 --> 00:01:42,039
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes flattened in the sense that you could see the clearly, like, the first paragraph says, should there arise desire, or greed, or hate, i.e.

12
00:01:42,159 --> 00:01:46,280
[SPEAKER_00]: aversion, or delusion, or even a patiga.

13
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:48,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's different than hate.

14
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:49,300
[SPEAKER_00]: It's different than aversion.

15
00:01:50,601 --> 00:01:51,841
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just translate it,

16
00:01:52,917 --> 00:02:00,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, greed, hate, delusion, or even any aversion, it kind of becomes like a poetic sort of... and it completely loses the depth.

17
00:02:02,201 --> 00:02:10,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the patīgā of their cetasā, not just, oh, some slight aversion, because somebody didn't put sugar in my coffee or something.

18
00:02:11,125 --> 00:02:13,186
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bit more phenomenological than that.

19
00:02:13,566 --> 00:02:18,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's kind of the point of why, like, sometimes it's good to leave certain things completely untranslated.

20
00:02:21,635 --> 00:02:37,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So it says, basically, so yes, if any of these things are found in the citta, stemming from it, being pushed forward by the citta, from that they should reign in the citta.

21
00:02:38,486 --> 00:02:39,427
[SPEAKER_00]: By thinking this.

22
00:02:40,568 --> 00:02:49,014
[SPEAKER_00]: This path is fearful, with terror, with thorns, with thickets, a wrong path, a bad path, difficult to travel.

23
00:02:49,414 --> 00:02:53,137
[SPEAKER_00]: This path of desire, of greed, of hate, of delusion, that path.

24
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:59,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this path is frequented by ignoble, āsapurisa.

25
00:02:59,662 --> 00:03:03,005
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the path frequented by the noble, sapurisa.

26
00:03:03,485 --> 00:03:05,266
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't lead to wisdom, basically.

27
00:03:05,987 --> 00:03:07,008
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not worthy of you.

28
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:23,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like a generic stock phrase to describe a type of reflection that you need to be bringing in front of your citta, in front of your mind, sustaining it, reminding it, like relentlessly reminding it of that.

29
00:03:24,949 --> 00:03:30,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, when the animal wants to go over the fence, you need to remind it why it is dangerous to do so.

30
00:03:31,753 --> 00:03:36,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it wants to go over the fence frequently, well, you frequently have to remind it.

31
00:03:37,309 --> 00:03:41,531
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not one and done.

32
00:03:44,672 --> 00:03:46,133
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it goes to repeat the same.

33
00:03:46,773 --> 00:03:57,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one should train the citta from forms cognizable by the eye, sight, sounds, smell, taste, touches, basically, ideas as well.

34
00:03:57,078 --> 00:03:58,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when mental

35
00:03:59,305 --> 00:04:13,517
[SPEAKER_00]: When thoughts, mental phenomena, images present themselves, you would see the citta wanting to leap towards them with lust, with greed, with hatred.

36
00:04:16,139 --> 00:04:17,060
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to rein it in.

37
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:25,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, say no and then remind it of the drawbacks, of the dangers of doing so and benefits of not doing so.

38
00:04:27,317 --> 00:04:28,097
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it repeats the same.

39
00:04:28,317 --> 00:04:30,838
[SPEAKER_00]: This path is fearful, with terror, with thorns, with thickets.

40
00:04:31,398 --> 00:04:33,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Thickets, a wrong path, a bad path, difficult to travel.

41
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:37,461
[SPEAKER_00]: So, frequented by ignoble, not frequented by the noble.

42
00:04:37,941 --> 00:04:38,461
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not worthy.

43
00:04:38,961 --> 00:04:40,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, why is it not worthy?

44
00:04:41,182 --> 00:04:46,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, anybody who wants to practice the Buddha's teaching,

45
00:04:47,455 --> 00:04:58,242
[SPEAKER_00]: You should be aligning, aspiring towards and working towards alignment with the values presented by the Buddha, because you yourself see the value in those values, so to speak.

46
00:04:58,922 --> 00:05:04,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you see the value in training, you have faith in it, so that you can be free from suffering.

47
00:05:04,806 --> 00:05:12,731
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a genuine, profound desire to be free from dukkha, free from liability, free from attachment, free from any danger.

48
00:05:13,656 --> 00:05:15,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a real thing.

49
00:05:15,436 --> 00:05:24,859
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's just like, if you forget that, then citta can pressure you back to things you know are an obstacle for what you actually want.

50
00:05:25,299 --> 00:05:28,980
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that, eh... What is it?

51
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:29,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Talaputta, yeah.

52
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:30,861
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it was Talaputta.

53
00:05:32,021 --> 00:05:39,083
[SPEAKER_00]: In that, eh... Terra Terrigata, when... when he was talking to his own citta back when he was in the forest.

54
00:05:39,981 --> 00:05:53,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... and saying basically, like, oh, before I became a monk, when I was a householder, my own mind was telling me, hey, leave this crowded and dusty life, go forth,

55
00:05:54,807 --> 00:05:58,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Enjoy the freedom of the wilderness, you know, practice for the freedom of suffering.

56
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:00,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was quite inspired, basically.

57
00:06:00,571 --> 00:06:02,773
[SPEAKER_00]: His own mind was quite inspired to practice.

58
00:06:03,513 --> 00:06:04,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then he did it.

59
00:06:04,574 --> 00:06:10,978
[SPEAKER_00]: He went forth, then went to the forest, and now the mind was like, whoa, yeah, you know, we did this, like, now I want something else.

60
00:06:12,159 --> 00:06:17,203
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then he wanted to go back, and then he was saying to the mind, like, hold on, but you were the one that brought me here.

61
00:06:19,191 --> 00:06:23,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you want to go back to that which you yourself said was not worthy and everything else.

62
00:06:23,414 --> 00:06:24,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then he's like, no.

63
00:06:24,935 --> 00:06:30,459
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm gonna make you sit here now with me, and we're gonna work through this, and you're not going back.

64
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:33,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, he worked through it and became an arahant.

65
00:06:36,804 --> 00:06:43,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says like, oh, suppose because there was a rich crop, the crop guardian is negligent,

66
00:06:44,723 --> 00:06:52,209
[SPEAKER_00]: And a crop-eating ox, having entered that crop, would indulge himself in intoxication and negligence as much as he wants.

67
00:06:53,410 --> 00:07:13,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Even so, because an uninstructed whirling, asutava, putujana, acting without restraint in the six bases of pressure, passa, I translated it as pressure, well, most of you already know that, indulges in intoxication and negligence as much as he wants in the five strands of sensual pleasure.

68
00:07:15,253 --> 00:07:32,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of... even pertains to somebody who say... who's just at the very beginning of the practice, which would be the the asūtava, the uninstructed worldling, who might have started learning some instruction.

69
00:07:33,769 --> 00:07:38,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, allowing the mind, the citta,

70
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:55,854
[SPEAKER_00]: To enter the crop, indulge itself in the crop as much as it wants, with intoxication and negligence, towards the sensuality, the five strands of sensual pleasure, equals what the previous paragraph just described.

71
00:07:55,974 --> 00:08:02,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Equals following the ignoble path that takes away from wisdom, that obstructs the wisdom, and does not lead to freedom from suffering.

72
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:04,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of pretty self-evident, really.

73
00:08:05,649 --> 00:08:08,010
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it doesn't matter how self-evident it is.

74
00:08:08,511 --> 00:08:17,055
[SPEAKER_00]: When the pressure is there, to act out of the greed, desire and so on, eh... the context is lost.

75
00:08:17,215 --> 00:08:24,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, that's why, like, you have to remind yourself of what you actually want, what you actually value, and then rein in the mind.

76
00:08:24,759 --> 00:08:30,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, that's the principle of that training, is, like, from asuttava putujana up to the arahantship.

77
00:08:30,762 --> 00:08:33,264
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the part that doesn't change whatsoever.

78
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:42,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the next... let me just scroll this up, yeah...

79
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:45,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the next paragraph is the opposite.

80
00:08:46,807 --> 00:08:48,388
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, teach him a lesson.

81
00:08:48,408 --> 00:08:49,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, you don't go there.

82
00:08:49,669 --> 00:08:51,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, we've been through this before.

83
00:08:51,430 --> 00:08:53,831
[SPEAKER_00]: We rationalized many times.

84
00:09:16,506 --> 00:09:21,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Both you and I know that that this is the the ignoble path, the obstruction.

85
00:09:22,310 --> 00:09:24,031
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just there's no more discussion, in other words.

86
00:09:24,091 --> 00:09:26,152
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like grab you by the nose and pull you back.

87
00:09:27,893 --> 00:09:30,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then even even the sutta describes.

88
00:09:30,175 --> 00:09:31,616
[SPEAKER_00]: And then second time the ox does it.

89
00:09:31,636 --> 00:09:32,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the third time.

90
00:09:32,496 --> 00:09:34,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the hundred and third time does it.

91
00:09:35,978 --> 00:09:36,919
[SPEAKER_00]: You repeat the same.

92
00:09:37,359 --> 00:09:38,520
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you are not going there.

93
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:39,841
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not fueling

94
00:09:40,746 --> 00:09:43,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither that attitude, nor that behavior.

95
00:09:43,467 --> 00:09:46,368
[SPEAKER_00]: You can kick and scream as much as you want, like a spoiled brat.

96
00:09:47,068 --> 00:09:50,089
[SPEAKER_00]: If you give in, you convey the wrong message.

97
00:09:51,249 --> 00:09:52,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And by giving in, I mean that.

98
00:09:52,410 --> 00:09:54,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, choose to go down that path.

99
00:09:55,030 --> 00:10:02,433
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, oh, giving in doesn't mean having your mind... having your mind have a desire.

100
00:10:03,293 --> 00:10:06,594
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not you giving in.

101
00:10:06,694 --> 00:10:08,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind getting angry, citta getting angry.

102
00:10:09,015 --> 00:10:10,215
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not you giving in.

103
00:10:12,006 --> 00:10:18,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But you choosing to allow it, to act, you to act on account of it, that is you giving in.

104
00:10:19,351 --> 00:10:24,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why you don't need to, as I said, strangle or kill your mind or not perceive anything to be free from it.

105
00:10:24,735 --> 00:10:32,740
[SPEAKER_00]: You just not be the one, you just not, you must not be the one who would be making the choice of, yeah, okay, go ahead into the crop.

106
00:10:34,571 --> 00:10:39,417
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is not that the animal wants to go back to the crop and eat as much as it wants and so on.

107
00:10:39,437 --> 00:10:41,159
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you keep letting it.

108
00:10:41,679 --> 00:10:42,040
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

109
00:10:42,907 --> 00:10:44,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that line of the restraint.

110
00:10:44,708 --> 00:10:53,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't need to torment yourself, you don't need to sleep on the bed of nails and stuff like that to just somehow magically cure the mind from desires.

111
00:10:53,872 --> 00:11:08,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, you just need to be... to recognize that you must not be the one willingly, consciously making the choice that affirms sensuality, greed, aversion, distraction and so on.

112
00:11:09,238 --> 00:11:10,279
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the middle path.

113
00:11:10,319 --> 00:11:15,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that and then the practice will not necessarily be unpleasant.

114
00:11:17,245 --> 00:11:23,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, like you just... yeah, the mind comes like this, the mind sometimes comes up like that.

115
00:11:23,171 --> 00:11:24,532
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the line I won't cross.

116
00:11:25,252 --> 00:11:27,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else outside of that line is not my problem.

117
00:11:28,275 --> 00:11:30,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like a lot of burden comes down from you.

118
00:11:35,338 --> 00:11:43,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, it compares this bringing back of the ox, compares, the Buddha compares that to a monk taming his cheetah.

119
00:11:45,805 --> 00:11:56,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even so, the paragraph says, for a bhikkhu, the cheetah is tamed, well tamed, regarding the six bases of pressure, in other words, the six types of crops.

120
00:11:57,314 --> 00:11:58,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the animal learned its lesson.

121
00:11:59,055 --> 00:12:00,316
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not letting it go there.

122
00:12:01,153 --> 00:12:05,294
[SPEAKER_00]: You bring it back, you present it with perceptions and you're diligent in that regard.

123
00:12:06,674 --> 00:12:13,255
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't go around complaining, feeling sorry for yourself because your mind still wants these things.

124
00:12:13,275 --> 00:12:18,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, it's like literally complaining that, well, the dog still wants to go and chew the bone.

125
00:12:19,077 --> 00:12:22,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, what a surprise.

126
00:12:22,637 --> 00:12:24,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Still wants to go and chase other animals.

127
00:12:24,398 --> 00:12:25,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, what a surprise.

128
00:12:25,818 --> 00:12:26,978
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the animal does.

129
00:12:27,098 --> 00:12:27,998
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what citta does.

130
00:12:29,639 --> 00:12:30,059
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like,

131
00:12:30,725 --> 00:12:31,665
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's not my problem.

132
00:12:31,685 --> 00:12:34,906
[SPEAKER_00]: My problem is if I'm the one opening the gate and then paying the price for it.

133
00:12:35,526 --> 00:12:37,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll stop doing that and the annual will come out.

134
00:12:37,846 --> 00:12:38,986
[SPEAKER_00]: How long it takes to come down?

135
00:12:39,006 --> 00:12:41,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we'll just have to see once that happens.

136
00:12:45,208 --> 00:12:54,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, even so, because when for a bhikkhu the citta is tamed, well-tamed regarding the six bases of pressure, six types of crops, it remains centered within.

137
00:12:55,889 --> 00:12:59,630
[SPEAKER_00]: It settles down, it becomes unified, it enters samādhi.

138
00:13:00,874 --> 00:13:10,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the mind is tamed, when citta is tamed in regard to the six bases of contact, how?

139
00:13:10,298 --> 00:13:17,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha just described in the same sense how a shepherd would use a stick to prevent the ox from going into the wrong crops.

140
00:13:18,281 --> 00:13:20,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That is how.

141
00:13:20,302 --> 00:13:21,862
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't be any clearer than that.

142
00:13:21,942 --> 00:13:23,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Really, even the simile is not technical.

143
00:13:25,628 --> 00:13:28,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the mind settles down and becomes unified.

144
00:13:28,630 --> 00:13:30,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you enter samādhi.

145
00:13:30,451 --> 00:13:31,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you develop samādhi.

146
00:13:33,592 --> 00:13:37,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no samādhi, it's because there is no right training in regard to the crop.

147
00:13:37,834 --> 00:13:45,058
[SPEAKER_00]: There is still that allowing the oxygen to go into the crop as much as you want, despite what you might be telling yourself or what you might be believing.

148
00:13:52,707 --> 00:14:00,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it goes into kind of the second half, giving a simile in regarding to the lute and the king.

149
00:14:01,413 --> 00:14:08,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose because the sound of a lute was never heard before by a king or a royal minister.

150
00:14:10,199 --> 00:14:15,102
[SPEAKER_00]: He might hear a sound of the lute and say, good man, whose sound is this?

151
00:14:15,303 --> 00:14:20,266
[SPEAKER_00]: So enchanting, so desirable, so intoxicating, so infatuating, so captivating.

152
00:14:21,202 --> 00:14:26,991
[SPEAKER_00]: They would say to him, well, this, sir, is the... that which is called the lute, the sound of which is so enchanting and captivating.

153
00:14:29,194 --> 00:14:31,017
[SPEAKER_00]: He would then say, go, man, bring me that lute.

154
00:14:31,197 --> 00:14:32,019
[SPEAKER_00]: They would bring him the lute.

155
00:14:32,940 --> 00:14:35,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would say, enough of it, bring me just the sound.

156
00:14:37,197 --> 00:14:42,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they would explain to him that this luta is made of many components, of great components.

157
00:14:42,138 --> 00:14:51,721
[SPEAKER_00]: It speaks, it speaks having dependent on many components, depending on the parchment, the belly, the arm, the bridge, the strings, the plectrum, depending on the appropriate effort of a person.

158
00:14:53,841 --> 00:14:58,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, this luta, sir, is, or sire, is made of many components.

159
00:14:59,162 --> 00:15:01,303
[SPEAKER_00]: It speaks having dependent on many components.

160
00:15:03,292 --> 00:15:07,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the man, the king, would choose to split the loot into ten or hundred pieces.

161
00:15:07,193 --> 00:15:08,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Having split it, he would splinter it.

162
00:15:08,693 --> 00:15:11,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Having splintered it, he would burn it with fire and make it into ashes.

163
00:15:11,153 --> 00:15:16,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Having made it into ashes, he would winnow it in a strong wind and let it be carried away by a swift flowing river.

164
00:15:18,515 --> 00:15:21,615
[SPEAKER_00]: He would say, a poor thing indeed is this so-called loot.

165
00:15:21,655 --> 00:15:26,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever a loot may be, herein, the people are excessively negligent and deceived.

166
00:15:26,776 --> 00:15:32,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Meaning, he destroyed the loot, trying to get to the sound, and there was no sound.

167
00:15:33,930 --> 00:15:35,110
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he couldn't find the sound.

168
00:15:35,910 --> 00:15:40,791
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll get to that part when we do the questions later, as to why this is relevant to the first part.

169
00:15:42,852 --> 00:15:57,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, even so, because Abhikūr investigates form, as far as the designation or destination of form goes, he investigates feeling, he investigates perception, he investigates determinations, he investigates consciousness, yes.

170
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:08,489
[SPEAKER_00]: As he investigates form, feeling, perception, determination, consciousness, as far as the destination of consciousness goes, whatever there was of I am, of mine, that for him is no more.

171
00:16:10,031 --> 00:16:14,355
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

172
00:16:14,495 --> 00:16:22,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that whole kind of... the second part is just to illustrate, let's say, like an inside view of

173
00:16:24,702 --> 00:16:33,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the desire that you're taming through practicing the first part of the sutta, which is bringing the oxen back from the crop.

174
00:16:33,989 --> 00:16:35,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Bringing the oxen back from the crop.

175
00:16:37,612 --> 00:16:49,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the few questions that I compiled, they kind of, sort of, try to bridge the theory and the practice between this, in this sutta.

176
00:16:50,994 --> 00:16:59,363
[SPEAKER_00]: was the the question of the the leaking fence, or the not properly closed gate, or whatever similar comparison you want to use.

177
00:17:00,564 --> 00:17:05,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the simile of the ox, the guardian only strikes the ox when it enters the crop.

178
00:17:06,330 --> 00:17:09,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't go around beating it all the time, if it's not doing anything wrong.

179
00:17:10,234 --> 00:17:11,776
[SPEAKER_00]: What is wrong in this context?

180
00:17:11,836 --> 00:17:13,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the sutta clearly describes it.

181
00:17:15,338 --> 00:17:20,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual desire is wrong, greed is wrong, hatred, anger is wrong, and so on.

182
00:17:22,564 --> 00:17:36,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in one's daily life, can you discern your citta as something that's already leaning towards those things?

183
00:17:36,594 --> 00:17:45,021
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, can you notice when the animal is already inclining or planning to try and sneak through the gate?

184
00:17:47,343 --> 00:17:51,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you only see it when it's right there and you're trying to, like, drag it back from the wrong crop?

185
00:17:53,767 --> 00:17:55,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the important factor.

186
00:17:55,087 --> 00:18:00,750
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like if you're waiting for the bull to be entering the crop to start paying attention, it's too late.

187
00:18:01,471 --> 00:18:02,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, it's too late.

188
00:18:03,392 --> 00:18:12,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to start noticing the tendencies of your mind way before it comes into, like, a full-blown

189
00:18:13,590 --> 00:18:18,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, now I just have to be beating it with a stick and pulling it by the nose and it's really hard because it weighs a ton.

190
00:18:21,594 --> 00:18:31,238
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like... Obviously, it's like a very important consideration, but it's also something, as I said, it's like you have to...

191
00:18:31,338 --> 00:18:32,599
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna have layers to it.

192
00:18:32,719 --> 00:18:40,562
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you know, you might think you're noticing the... the... the background state of the animal.

193
00:18:40,602 --> 00:18:42,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is my mind leaning and so on?

194
00:18:43,200 --> 00:19:00,290
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you can know whether you're, say, correctly or accurately enough doing that noticing, if when the animal is at the doorstep of the crop, are you able to actually pull it back?

195
00:19:00,530 --> 00:19:02,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you able to not let it go that way?

196
00:19:04,090 --> 00:19:07,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, like, okay, we're just eating in the crop now and then I'll start again later.

197
00:19:08,532 --> 00:19:15,455
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's the case, if that's the case more often than not, means you are not noticing the state of the animal early enough.

198
00:19:15,835 --> 00:19:27,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you gotta start... you gotta stop distracting yourself with other seemingly important things and look backwards, peripheral, at the background of your mind, what kind of

199
00:19:31,167 --> 00:19:36,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Sort of, like, moods, inclinations are there, leaning, shaping up.

200
00:19:36,273 --> 00:19:39,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what kind of clouds are coming up the horizon?

201
00:19:39,276 --> 00:19:44,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Way before you are hit by a storm.

202
00:19:44,522 --> 00:19:50,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is the reason why the animal is still able... why you're not able to pull the animal out of the crop, basically.

203
00:19:50,309 --> 00:19:51,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... it's too late.

204
00:19:55,122 --> 00:20:01,728
[SPEAKER_00]: The second question is about the Sutta describing the whole path of following desire or aversion.

205
00:20:03,449 --> 00:20:09,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, desire as sensual desire, aversion as ill will, hatred, acting out.

206
00:20:09,454 --> 00:20:13,437
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all on the same plane, pertains to the world, so to speak.

207
00:20:13,458 --> 00:20:17,741
[SPEAKER_00]: The Sutta describes it as the path which is like a path covered with thorns.

208
00:20:18,902 --> 00:20:21,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Dangerous path where you're just gonna injure yourself.

209
00:20:23,508 --> 00:20:35,172
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's this... the question is, like, when the... when that patīgā, that, as I said, the natural, the fundamental, the underlying resistance toward your own senses... Well, resistance of citta towards your own senses.

210
00:20:35,472 --> 00:20:37,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty technical.

211
00:20:38,773 --> 00:20:41,433
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's just a neutral patīgā...

212
00:20:42,674 --> 00:20:45,055
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still a resistance, by the way, so it can never be entirely neutral.

213
00:20:45,635 --> 00:20:49,776
[SPEAKER_00]: But what I'm saying, that is, patīgā is not necessarily aversion.

214
00:20:50,536 --> 00:20:52,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily hatred or real will.

215
00:20:53,626 --> 00:20:57,832
[SPEAKER_00]: It's where hatred or ill will finds its basis from.

216
00:20:57,852 --> 00:20:58,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

217
00:20:58,272 --> 00:21:06,343
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the same sense how a sign of subha, the sign of beautiful agreeable shape, form, sound, doesn't matter.

218
00:21:06,823 --> 00:21:10,328
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not where sensuality is, but it's where sensuality finds its base from.

219
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:25,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's as subtle as that neutral patīgā, that resistance that you just have towards pretty much your own physicality, naturally, because senses are a burden, they offer inertia, that inertia is patīgā.

220
00:21:26,347 --> 00:21:31,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, but whether that patīgā becomes your own aversion, that's a secondary thing.

221
00:21:32,331 --> 00:21:36,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question here, it's like, how far can you go back and notice

222
00:21:37,407 --> 00:21:43,050
[SPEAKER_00]: The precursors to your desire or real will, the the clouds, they're coming up the horizon.

223
00:21:44,450 --> 00:21:48,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you need to, like, have them right in front of your face to become aware of them?

224
00:21:48,732 --> 00:22:00,678
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, it's because, most likely, you're probably distracting yourself too much with other things and not investing enough time to contemplate, sit quietly with this and think it, like,

225
00:22:01,279 --> 00:22:05,065
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, invoke it, remember it, recollect it, try to clarify it, and so on.

226
00:22:05,926 --> 00:22:08,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And then see it apply their life, so to speak.

227
00:22:08,410 --> 00:22:13,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, okay, so what is the current, the widest possible horizon of my current mood?

228
00:22:13,297 --> 00:22:13,718
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

229
00:22:15,687 --> 00:22:17,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that whole, like a horizon...

230
00:22:17,729 --> 00:22:22,713
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I remember it was Nyanavira and Nyanamoli discussing it in the early letters.

231
00:22:22,753 --> 00:22:24,034
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like...

232
00:22:24,634 --> 00:22:33,001
[SPEAKER_00]: They were describing relationship, if I remember correctly, between citta and cetanā as like a horizon and things arising from that horizon.

233
00:22:33,041 --> 00:22:38,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like it sets up the direction and then things arise from there.

234
00:22:38,165 --> 00:22:44,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's... it's like... it's important for that phenomenon to be... to... well...

235
00:22:45,424 --> 00:22:53,731
[SPEAKER_00]: To not be as theoretical for people, but to actually start to relate to it in your own practical, like, experience, day-to-day experience as a whole.

236
00:22:54,592 --> 00:22:55,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is there.

237
00:22:55,492 --> 00:23:07,062
[SPEAKER_00]: All these moods and precursors and wide, kind of, as I said, very, very peripheral leaning and preferences are there and they are leaning you and setting you up for what's to follow out of them.

238
00:23:07,992 --> 00:23:17,316
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sooner you spot them there, obviously, the less kind of physical effort you will have to apply onto the bull to pull him back.

239
00:23:18,196 --> 00:23:21,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

240
00:23:22,257 --> 00:23:22,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

241
00:23:24,718 --> 00:23:27,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, now the whole simile of the lute.

242
00:23:28,120 --> 00:23:35,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Sister Iveka mentioned in her reply before, it's like a simile compared to a rainbow, which I think is quite a good simile.

243
00:23:36,764 --> 00:23:45,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the king wants the sound of the lute, but ignores the existing, presently, simultaneously existing unity of its parts.

244
00:23:45,988 --> 00:23:58,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can have the strings, you can have the body, you could have the musician, but if you have them separate, not unified in that particular arrangement, you will not have the sound of the lute.

245
00:23:59,255 --> 00:24:01,396
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same, exactly the same with the rainbow.

246
00:24:03,232 --> 00:24:05,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, does that mean rainbow is not real as such?

247
00:24:05,273 --> 00:24:08,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean sound of the lute is not real as such?

248
00:24:22,843 --> 00:24:23,483
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not at all.

249
00:24:23,503 --> 00:24:31,606
[SPEAKER_00]: There would be a lazy way out, saying, oh, because you can take it apart, it doesn't exist in some sort of imaginable reality.

250
00:24:32,266 --> 00:24:34,827
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just basic kind of contradiction in terms.

251
00:24:35,468 --> 00:24:39,989
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's quite an irrational standpoint, which is why mysticism comes out of it.

252
00:24:40,890 --> 00:24:41,730
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it is real as such.

253
00:24:41,890 --> 00:24:44,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that the lute has a pleasant sound.

254
00:24:46,192 --> 00:24:49,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But the sound, it's not found in any of the specific components.

255
00:24:51,568 --> 00:24:54,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that I am factually seeing the rainbow.

256
00:24:54,649 --> 00:24:57,170
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I can describe the colors, I can even describe the size.

257
00:24:57,210 --> 00:24:58,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there's a double one, look.

258
00:24:59,171 --> 00:24:59,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, so what?

259
00:24:59,911 --> 00:25:00,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that not real?

260
00:25:00,711 --> 00:25:01,231
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's real.

261
00:25:02,452 --> 00:25:06,413
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, if I look for it where it isn't, it's not going to be there.

262
00:25:07,673 --> 00:25:08,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is this relevant?

263
00:25:08,834 --> 00:25:10,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, desire is the same.

264
00:25:12,175 --> 00:25:13,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will is the same.

265
00:25:16,836 --> 00:25:18,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much any pressure

266
00:25:19,290 --> 00:25:21,771
[SPEAKER_00]: The citta exerts on you, it's the same nature.

267
00:25:22,592 --> 00:25:33,318
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you see the rainbow, the experience of it, the natural pressure of unenlightened mind is to push you towards it.

268
00:25:33,398 --> 00:25:34,318
[SPEAKER_00]: But towards what?

269
00:25:35,439 --> 00:25:41,242
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there when you are not close or too close to it, so to speak, or when you're not too far.

270
00:25:41,322 --> 00:25:43,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's there the way it is there.

271
00:25:43,864 --> 00:25:47,946
[SPEAKER_00]: But the tendency of the mind is to push you forward, to push you for more of it.

272
00:25:48,564 --> 00:25:50,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't know where to look for more of it.

273
00:25:50,465 --> 00:25:51,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, where do you look?

274
00:25:51,085 --> 00:25:52,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you look among the sense objects.

275
00:25:52,506 --> 00:25:57,628
[SPEAKER_00]: You look among the forms, the feeling, all the other palpable things.

276
00:25:57,668 --> 00:26:04,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, after the Simile of the Loot, the Buddha started describing the aggregates, how when the monk starts looking at the aggregates, he won't find the sense of I am there.

277
00:26:05,232 --> 00:26:07,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the sense of I am shares the same nature.

278
00:26:09,154 --> 00:26:11,235
[SPEAKER_00]: But on a more... well...

279
00:26:12,197 --> 00:26:24,047
[SPEAKER_00]: On a more practical level, for somebody, say, who is kind of still working on that training the ox, you could see exactly the same principle revealed in just the essential desire.

280
00:26:24,167 --> 00:26:32,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm pretty sure, like, everybody, with anyone, with some degree of self-awareness, but, say, outside of the context of the Buddhist practice, outside of the suttas,

281
00:26:34,019 --> 00:26:40,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Deep down, has a recognition, because it has happened so many times, you have a desire towards something.

282
00:26:40,725 --> 00:26:54,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You have that pressure towards something perceived, currently, as a kind of dependent on the unified components of your experience, to put it that way, on sights, eyes, desire, this and that.

283
00:26:54,397 --> 00:27:00,402
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, that kind of pressure, it's real as such, to that extent.

284
00:27:01,249 --> 00:27:03,051
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what would people naturally do?

285
00:27:03,091 --> 00:27:05,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then, they would go for more of the sights, more of this.

286
00:27:06,353 --> 00:27:10,997
[SPEAKER_00]: But they themselves would then experience that, oh, well, this was a bit of a letdown.

287
00:27:12,478 --> 00:27:13,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't find it there.

288
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:15,281
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't find those things there.

289
00:27:15,361 --> 00:27:16,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with the food.

290
00:27:17,483 --> 00:27:26,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it's that, pretty much, joy of anticipation is where your happiness, what your happiness is bound with.

291
00:27:27,205 --> 00:27:31,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you start eating or consuming or doing this, you went too far.

292
00:27:33,926 --> 00:27:40,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And you just find yourself, okay, desire is gone, because the phenomenon of rainbow is gone, because I went too far.

293
00:27:41,028 --> 00:27:44,268
[SPEAKER_00]: The angle changed, there are no more water droplets there or whatever.

294
00:27:45,829 --> 00:27:52,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why each time, it sets you up from the beginning, so to speak, to fail in exactly the same way.

295
00:27:55,117 --> 00:27:57,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're looking for it where it can't be found.

296
00:27:59,438 --> 00:28:05,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, the same principle of just the basic core central desire that always sets you towards sense objects.

297
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:19,083
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I was saying that a long time ago in those talks from Sri Lanka, it's like... A mere thought of seeing or touching or tasting something already produces, like,

298
00:28:20,385 --> 00:28:23,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Excitement, dissipation, desire, pressure, even more.

299
00:28:23,847 --> 00:28:24,928
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like... but hold on.

300
00:28:25,988 --> 00:28:39,076
[SPEAKER_00]: If, through a common asutava-putujana belief, pleasure is in touching, tasting, seeing, smelling, listening, and so on, how can you already experience the pleasure way before you are in contact with those things?

301
00:28:40,531 --> 00:28:45,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Way before you even went and kind of sought it, and found it, and looked at it, and ate it, and so on.

302
00:28:46,216 --> 00:28:47,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you're experiencing the same.

303
00:28:48,477 --> 00:28:54,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, if it's truly in those things, you could only experience it when you are actively in contact with them.

304
00:28:56,424 --> 00:28:56,964
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the point.

305
00:28:57,004 --> 00:28:58,546
[SPEAKER_00]: It was never in those things.

306
00:28:58,626 --> 00:29:07,093
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the more you are ignoring the whole necessity of restraint, necessity of training in virtue, necessity of withdrawing the ox,

307
00:29:08,317 --> 00:29:18,709
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you are reinforcing the belief that, as I said, even a sutta puthujjana, when he's honest with himself, knows that, yeah, things are not really satisfying at the end of the day.

308
00:29:18,749 --> 00:29:19,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, doesn't matter how much...

309
00:29:19,750 --> 00:29:27,318
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I forget about that, and then I continue to kind of distract myself with the thrill of the chase.

310
00:29:29,837 --> 00:29:33,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like chasing the the rainbows while I know I can't find them.

311
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:35,721
[SPEAKER_00]: But, anyway, I ignore that part.

312
00:29:36,401 --> 00:29:38,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, deactivation is what gives me joy.

313
00:29:40,212 --> 00:29:41,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why this simile is relevant.

314
00:29:41,672 --> 00:29:44,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Relevant to the whole restraint and everything.

315
00:29:44,253 --> 00:29:48,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like in the in the beginning, yes, you have to just whack the bull and pull it back and not let it go there.

316
00:29:48,595 --> 00:29:53,196
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eventually, you have to start seeing through the whole pattern.

317
00:29:53,236 --> 00:29:53,916
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, hold on.

318
00:29:54,356 --> 00:29:55,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, there is pressure.

319
00:29:55,877 --> 00:30:00,498
[SPEAKER_00]: But, how many times, like even before the restraint and after restraint, I know it's not in those things.

320
00:30:01,198 --> 00:30:02,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, my citta needs to know that.

321
00:30:03,059 --> 00:30:05,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why these perceptions of the danger and so on.

322
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:06,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're the same knowledge.

323
00:30:07,650 --> 00:30:11,431
[SPEAKER_00]: The obviousness of it will sink to the mind, down to the mind.

324
00:30:12,751 --> 00:30:16,392
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it does so, you don't have to be pulling the oxen back.

325
00:30:17,232 --> 00:30:21,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't go there, because there is nothing there to find from the beginning.

326
00:30:21,613 --> 00:30:24,394
[SPEAKER_00]: What you thought it was getting from those things, it wasn't there.

327
00:30:25,694 --> 00:30:26,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the sole problem.

328
00:30:26,694 --> 00:30:30,375
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a sole deception of sensuality and stuff, all the similes that we talked about.

329
00:30:31,455 --> 00:30:35,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if it were not a deception, it would not have been unwholesome.

330
00:30:36,138 --> 00:30:37,498
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's actually another sutta, isn't it?

331
00:30:37,518 --> 00:30:45,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Where the Buddha says, māra, oh, sorry, hunter does not set up a trap for the welfare of the animal.

332
00:30:46,802 --> 00:30:48,402
[SPEAKER_00]: It just cannot be done for that purpose.

333
00:30:48,822 --> 00:30:54,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a hunter does not set up a trap, because he sets up the trap to get the animal, to get the meat, to hunt you down.

334
00:30:55,725 --> 00:30:58,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, you can't go around, you can't see a hunter.

335
00:30:59,306 --> 00:31:02,907
[SPEAKER_00]: But imagine yourself entering a forest and you see something that

336
00:31:04,147 --> 00:31:05,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Really looks like a trap.

337
00:31:07,451 --> 00:31:13,778
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, hmm, this looks like it's trying to get me to go there for whatever reason.

338
00:31:17,013 --> 00:31:25,995
[SPEAKER_00]: A man with, like, normal rational mind and a normal, as I said, common sense wisdom, would, as such a man, choose to go there, or was like, no?

339
00:31:26,395 --> 00:31:27,915
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I probably wouldn't.

340
00:31:28,135 --> 00:31:28,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why would I?

341
00:31:29,235 --> 00:31:37,477
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, the way... like, if something needs to be set up to get you there first, it's something you naturally wouldn't trust.

342
00:31:37,897 --> 00:31:39,997
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's exactly how sensuality is set up.

343
00:31:42,077 --> 00:31:45,518
[SPEAKER_00]: It's set up to get you there first, and then the person's like, but hold on.

344
00:31:46,409 --> 00:31:56,193
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm already feeling the pressure before I even got there, means that's already a setup that has nothing to do with things I'm trying to get to.

345
00:31:59,975 --> 00:32:01,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would I go there?

346
00:32:02,016 --> 00:32:03,576
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't be for my welfare.

347
00:32:03,916 --> 00:32:09,719
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody set this up in this manner, it cannot be for my welfare, because it's deceitful.

348
00:32:13,165 --> 00:32:14,926
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one of the dangers of sensuality.

349
00:32:15,467 --> 00:32:17,628
[SPEAKER_00]: All the other implications that you trapped yourself in.

350
00:32:19,950 --> 00:32:26,614
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Sutta says, the ox eventually stops entering the crop, because it... well, it learned from the stick.

351
00:32:27,815 --> 00:32:32,598
[SPEAKER_00]: It learned from your relentless recollection of the right perception, sustaining the context at the right time,

352
00:32:33,484 --> 00:32:38,585
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you don't have to go around reciting the danger of sensuality 24-7 as some sort of mantra.

353
00:32:38,985 --> 00:32:39,125
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

354
00:32:39,245 --> 00:32:46,666
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you recognize that you are being pressured towards choosing sensuality or ill-will, that's when you bring up those contexts.

355
00:32:47,026 --> 00:32:47,626
[SPEAKER_00]: No exception.

356
00:32:48,447 --> 00:32:49,107
[SPEAKER_00]: No slacking.

357
00:32:49,807 --> 00:32:51,247
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind doesn't come up with it, great.

358
00:32:51,427 --> 00:32:52,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Put your feet up and relax.

359
00:32:55,268 --> 00:33:00,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question here was like, do you... like, do you do these such reflections?

360
00:33:00,449 --> 00:33:01,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you... do you remember yourself?

361
00:33:02,729 --> 00:33:02,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Like,

362
00:33:05,212 --> 00:33:14,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Times in the past, when you allowed sensuality, when you acted out of ill-will and so on, well, was it pleasant?

363
00:33:14,694 --> 00:33:15,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Was it beneficial?

364
00:33:16,294 --> 00:33:18,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Who was the main benefactor of it?

365
00:33:18,495 --> 00:33:19,695
[SPEAKER_00]: You or the wild animal?

366
00:33:19,835 --> 00:33:22,236
[SPEAKER_00]: They get you to do it.

367
00:33:22,276 --> 00:33:22,636
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

368
00:33:23,116 --> 00:33:28,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognizing those implicitly kind of unpleasant ways,

369
00:33:29,583 --> 00:33:39,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you recollect that as well, the emotional side of those things, to make it, as I said, more pertinent of a reflection, rather than just theoretically, as I said, recite through danger.

370
00:33:39,166 --> 00:33:41,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, feel the danger, in other words.

371
00:33:42,668 --> 00:33:43,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Feel the drawbacks.

372
00:33:46,249 --> 00:33:47,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So, make the connection.

373
00:33:47,329 --> 00:33:51,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Make the connection between the drawbacks of things that followed.

374
00:33:51,571 --> 00:33:55,612
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you could say like, oh, I don't see a connection between, like, giving sensuality and then something that's happening.

375
00:33:55,632 --> 00:33:58,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but something else that can happen.

376
00:33:59,370 --> 00:33:59,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Unfavorable.

377
00:33:59,911 --> 00:34:00,511
[SPEAKER_00]: That can happen.

378
00:34:01,112 --> 00:34:05,537
[SPEAKER_00]: And you know your mind will kick and scream and suffer and try and bite you as a result and you would suffer.

379
00:34:05,578 --> 00:34:07,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that is because that mind is untrained.

380
00:34:08,121 --> 00:34:09,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that mind untrained?

381
00:34:09,242 --> 00:34:14,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you've been allowing it into sensuality and into ill-will carelessly.

382
00:34:14,688 --> 00:34:15,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a connection.

383
00:34:19,465 --> 00:34:32,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to not forget it, and when you forget it, you need to remember it, you need to clarify it and protect it, so that next time there's sensuality, you see all the other collateral damage implied in it, so to speak.

384
00:34:36,501 --> 00:34:43,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, and then, in the end, the presence of the I am, of I am, that was, as I said, if you follow the same principle,

385
00:34:44,520 --> 00:34:55,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Of the rainbow existing real as such, or the sound of the lute being real as such, yet dependent on things that are not it, that are defining it.

386
00:34:55,750 --> 00:34:57,192
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same with the presence of I am.

387
00:34:57,372 --> 00:34:59,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the presence of I am is real as such.

388
00:34:59,414 --> 00:35:02,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of whoever you are is real as such.

389
00:35:02,056 --> 00:35:03,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an illusion.

390
00:35:03,918 --> 00:35:04,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an anatta.

391
00:35:05,019 --> 00:35:05,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing is real.

392
00:35:05,759 --> 00:35:06,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing is mine.

393
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:07,201
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

394
00:35:07,301 --> 00:35:08,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It's real as such.

395
00:35:08,221 --> 00:35:09,823
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a mistaken identity.

396
00:35:11,055 --> 00:35:14,037
[SPEAKER_00]: You think of it more than it actually is, to put it that way.

397
00:35:14,117 --> 00:35:23,684
[SPEAKER_00]: You think rainbow is over there, you know, 350 meters away, in those molecules, and if I go there and get it, I'll find it, I'll have it.

398
00:35:24,084 --> 00:35:29,067
[SPEAKER_00]: King thought sound of the lute was in the strings, so he started dissecting it and taking it apart.

399
00:35:31,249 --> 00:35:34,351
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, that's why, again, you can't dissect your own aggregates.

400
00:35:34,831 --> 00:35:35,572
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can try,

401
00:35:37,057 --> 00:35:44,823
[SPEAKER_00]: But the result would be absolutely destroyed loot and now without any capacity for the sound or understanding of the relationship between the two.

402
00:35:46,324 --> 00:35:56,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So that when the Buddha says you examine the form and stuff, you examine the nature of the form as that which kind of enables the sense of I am to depend on it.

403
00:35:58,353 --> 00:36:01,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And you just... you go... you don't need to go further than that.

404
00:36:01,454 --> 00:36:07,395
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to start tormenting, torturing, destroying... torturing, destroying your form, your feelings and so on, your consciousness.

405
00:36:07,435 --> 00:36:15,136
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I don't know how you would do that, but people might try, in order to prove yourself that... that a sense of I am is not yours.

406
00:36:15,336 --> 00:36:24,898
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you just need to basically stop thinking of yourself as independent of form, feeling, perception, determination and consciousness.

407
00:36:25,238 --> 00:36:25,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally, that's it.

408
00:36:29,170 --> 00:36:30,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop regarding yourself as the owner.

409
00:36:31,392 --> 00:36:37,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplate the impermanence to undermine the safety, the emotional safety of the sense of ownership.

410
00:36:37,936 --> 00:36:47,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just to, like, as I said, fall into a theoretical view that everything is constantly changing, therefore nothing is really mine, therefore everything is anatta, therefore the Buddha was right.

411
00:36:48,043 --> 00:36:48,924
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, no, no.

412
00:36:49,084 --> 00:36:52,967
[SPEAKER_00]: If you truly develop anatta, well, you'll be free from suffering.

413
00:36:53,973 --> 00:37:05,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the contemplation, the dissections, the analysis that the suttas describe, it's not mindless scientific scholarly analysis of random components, like how Abhidhamma or commentaries do it.

414
00:37:06,536 --> 00:37:13,657
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the actual contemplation of the live situation being undermined by something else that's there.

415
00:37:14,957 --> 00:37:17,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, oh, I'm hearing the sound of the lute.

416
00:37:18,334 --> 00:37:27,863
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not forgetting that it depends on these quite irrelevant, emotionally insignificant or even disgusting parts in and of themselves.

417
00:37:30,185 --> 00:37:35,150
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, you know, the contemplation of the body and the dangers and so on that other suttas talk about.

418
00:37:35,810 --> 00:37:39,413
[SPEAKER_00]: It's to just lose the desire towards the sound of the lute.

419
00:37:40,414 --> 00:37:44,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Lose the enchantment and the ridiculous idea of chasing the rainbow.

420
00:37:45,741 --> 00:38:04,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, become un-intoxicated with your own sense of I am, with your own self-view, sense of I am, conceit and everything else, by undermining it rightly, through recognition of things that do actually determine it, that is inseparable from and depends on.

421
00:38:06,134 --> 00:38:06,975
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

422
00:38:07,015 --> 00:38:08,236
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the sole purpose of this.

423
00:38:09,416 --> 00:38:13,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the difference between aversion and patiga?

424
00:38:14,019 --> 00:38:14,720
[SPEAKER_00]: They're all talks.

425
00:38:16,087 --> 00:38:19,289
[SPEAKER_00]: They talk about it more, so I don't want to be going into it.

426
00:38:22,272 --> 00:38:24,573
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I think I already covered it, didn't I?

427
00:38:25,374 --> 00:38:30,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, think of the resistance basically just as

428
00:38:31,429 --> 00:38:32,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Say there is... you're feeling neutral.

429
00:38:33,110 --> 00:38:33,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Say you're feeling neutral.

430
00:38:33,991 --> 00:38:37,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like nothing... nothing like bothering you or overtly pressuring you.

431
00:38:38,715 --> 00:38:41,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's no aversion, there's no desire, currently at least.

432
00:38:42,458 --> 00:38:44,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're just sitting.

433
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:49,364
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, there is a natural inertia of your existence as a whole.

434
00:38:49,384 --> 00:38:55,289
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a natural weight, you know, the body, the blob that you have to carry, the situation,

435
00:38:57,137 --> 00:39:06,461
[SPEAKER_00]: The heat, the cold, just all the little things are felt through a degree of their own, like, resistance, a degree of that, literally, like, in physics, inertia.

436
00:39:06,622 --> 00:39:07,182
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

437
00:39:07,222 --> 00:39:07,862
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good word.

438
00:39:08,102 --> 00:39:16,026
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, I think Jnanaviri and Molly were talking about translating rūpa as inertia, as that which offers inertia.

439
00:39:16,066 --> 00:39:17,187
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you know it's there.

440
00:39:19,088 --> 00:39:20,568
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the natural patīgā.

441
00:39:20,588 --> 00:39:26,191
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say patīgā, that in and of itself, it's a bit, as I said, weighty.

442
00:39:26,847 --> 00:39:27,327
[SPEAKER_00]: So to speak.

443
00:39:27,407 --> 00:39:28,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It has some weight on.

444
00:39:29,508 --> 00:39:32,970
[SPEAKER_00]: When that's not seen through, that is the basis.

445
00:39:34,491 --> 00:39:39,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's kind of the basis for even centrality, when you think about it.

446
00:39:40,014 --> 00:39:48,659
[SPEAKER_00]: It's certainly basis for aversion, but it's base for centrality as well, because it's... if it's not seen through, you will be discontent with it.

447
00:39:50,119 --> 00:39:54,040
[SPEAKER_00]: And discontent, as one of the Mahārāja's daughters, goes hand in hand with sensuality.

448
00:39:54,820 --> 00:40:14,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you are discontent, you will then be seeking, well, the only means of escape from that that you know, which is sensual pleasures, which is falling back into the belief that, if I follow the rainbow, I'll get to the source, the core, the substance, the center of it.

449
00:40:15,702 --> 00:40:22,184
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you just, as I said, deceive yourself with that repetitive ride.

450
00:40:22,985 --> 00:40:25,285
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that patiga is the base for it.

451
00:40:25,866 --> 00:40:29,647
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's actually that sutta... Well, I can't remember the sutta.

452
00:40:29,667 --> 00:40:40,871
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember the numbers anymore, but I do remember it was the... Arhita, yeah, when he said... when the Buddha asked, like, oh, who does the mindfulness of breathing here?

453
00:40:41,796 --> 00:40:42,436
[SPEAKER_00]: And how you do it?

454
00:40:42,637 --> 00:40:44,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And Narita said, I'm doing it.

455
00:40:44,058 --> 00:40:44,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Says, how?

456
00:40:44,458 --> 00:40:46,860
[SPEAKER_00]: I was like, well, mindfully I breathe in, mindfully I breathe out.

457
00:40:48,141 --> 00:40:49,722
[SPEAKER_00]: I know breath is there, or something like that.

458
00:40:50,162 --> 00:41:01,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, having abandoned desire for sense pleasures of the past, having abandoned desire for the sense pleasures of the future,

459
00:41:02,528 --> 00:41:07,169
[SPEAKER_00]: And the present, obviously, I'm abiding now free from internal patiga.

460
00:41:07,609 --> 00:41:14,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, not, obviously, seeing through the internal patiga, there is no more desire for any forms of pleasure, even the ones that I used to experience.

461
00:41:14,890 --> 00:41:18,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, yep, that is one way of doing ānāpānasati, but it's not complete.

462
00:41:19,511 --> 00:41:26,292
[SPEAKER_00]: And then went, well, the sutta goes into the stock phrase of, like, repeating the other, like, you know, I will breathe in like this, I will breathe in like that.

463
00:41:27,812 --> 00:41:31,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of showing that there are probably, there will be more nuances

464
00:41:32,433 --> 00:41:42,277
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't think that the suttas necessarily have preserved those nuances of descriptions, which is obviously a shame, but nothing we can do about it.

465
00:41:43,357 --> 00:41:50,440
[SPEAKER_00]: But pointing out that, okay, yeah, great, now do it even more and kind of completely uproot the sense of I am.

466
00:41:50,460 --> 00:41:54,642
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the result of fulfilling the ānāpānasati done rightly, the right recollection.

467
00:41:55,529 --> 00:42:07,519
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as Arita explained, it was like, the way he was describing it, you could see like, aha, seeing through patīgā internally, being free from it, by seeing through it, he has no desire towards sensual pleasures.

468
00:42:08,240 --> 00:42:09,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is exactly how it works.

469
00:42:09,861 --> 00:42:19,749
[SPEAKER_00]: If your own presence, neutral presence of your own senses, the weight, the inertia, is not bothersome to the citta, let alone you, your own animal does not mind them.

470
00:42:20,310 --> 00:42:22,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you would feel fairly light.

471
00:42:24,264 --> 00:42:27,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Fairly unburdened.

472
00:42:27,588 --> 00:42:28,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Fairly freed.

473
00:42:30,712 --> 00:42:31,112
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah.

474
00:42:31,353 --> 00:42:33,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be physically felt.

475
00:42:34,236 --> 00:42:36,699
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of the funny irony of it.

476
00:42:38,848 --> 00:42:49,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you won't be able to deal with that patiga if you're still having... acting out of sensuality, sexuality, coarse sensuality, ill will and so on.

477
00:42:49,652 --> 00:42:54,514
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like this... this... this is way too buried by those acts that are obviously rooted in it.

478
00:42:56,275 --> 00:43:01,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the... the second question was

479
00:43:04,585 --> 00:43:18,620
[SPEAKER_00]: The way to explain to the citta that aversion or sensuality is like a thorn is... well, one of the ways is as I suppose to contemplate the benefit of the peaceful neutral feeling.

480
00:43:18,861 --> 00:43:25,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes, if you contemplate the danger of following those pressures first.

481
00:43:28,577 --> 00:43:29,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to do this practically?

482
00:43:29,837 --> 00:43:31,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's how you do it practically.

483
00:43:31,477 --> 00:43:43,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if practically contemplating benefits of the neutral feeling are not quite doing it for the citta, it's not listening to you, well, then you have to practically contemplate the dangers of it.

484
00:43:43,261 --> 00:43:45,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Something a bit a bit heavier.

485
00:43:46,540 --> 00:43:49,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And when that kind of scares it back, then you show it what's actually... See?

486
00:43:50,503 --> 00:43:51,924
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't go there, this is what you get.

487
00:43:51,984 --> 00:43:53,205
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is actually beneficial.

488
00:43:53,905 --> 00:43:59,669
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're trying to show to a raging animal that staying calm is peaceful, it's not going to see it.

489
00:44:00,510 --> 00:44:05,433
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't really force the contemplation of the neutral feeling.

490
00:44:05,533 --> 00:44:10,457
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be... the peace of it needs to be discerned and recognized on account of not following

491
00:44:11,201 --> 00:44:20,207
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure... the pressure that comes out of the... the craving towards more pleasure, the craving to... to get rid of the aversion and pain and so on, and not acting out of those things.

492
00:44:20,328 --> 00:44:24,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So that... that... that is the only practical method that you can really do.

493
00:44:24,891 --> 00:44:31,956
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems... it seems to me that when I do allow the cow to eat from the crop that isn't supposed to, the line between me and the cow is blurred.

494
00:44:33,797 --> 00:44:36,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that because appropriation arises with allowing which increases the sense of I am?

495
00:44:38,080 --> 00:44:38,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

496
00:44:39,632 --> 00:44:42,112
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you see, the answer is really not relevant.

497
00:44:43,673 --> 00:44:47,173
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense, it doesn't matter why it happens.

498
00:44:48,094 --> 00:44:49,454
[SPEAKER_00]: You know exactly why it happens.

499
00:44:50,054 --> 00:44:54,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It happened because the choice you made allowed it to go there.

500
00:44:54,315 --> 00:44:55,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stop doing that.

501
00:44:55,735 --> 00:45:00,576
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to worry about appropriation and how it comes and why it works and how it works and so on.

502
00:45:01,696 --> 00:45:05,957
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it happens because, well, you lost perspective.

503
00:45:07,023 --> 00:45:13,508
[SPEAKER_00]: The shepherd that was supposed to keep the bull back, didn't, and then lost perspective.

504
00:45:13,588 --> 00:45:15,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, what do you do on account of it?

505
00:45:15,150 --> 00:45:16,711
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, do you wake up?

506
00:45:19,197 --> 00:45:19,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Acknowledge it.

507
00:45:20,618 --> 00:45:29,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Acknowledge the implications of it, the weight of the mistake, in order to help you to, you know, not fall back to it, into it, so easily as before.

508
00:45:30,024 --> 00:45:41,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Or does the shepherd then pretend, okay, well, let's pretend this never happened, won't happen again, next time I'll make a harder determination, and magically believe, and concentrate harder, and go on a longer retreat, and hopefully mind won't go there.

509
00:45:42,412 --> 00:45:43,033
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

510
00:45:44,572 --> 00:46:04,561
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind doesn't understand any of your token gestures, any of your observances, determinations and things that you magically are kind of trying to... hoping they will transfer the right perceptions down to the animal and they will just stop wanting those things.

511
00:46:05,061 --> 00:46:06,722
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's like the sutta is very clear.

512
00:46:08,462 --> 00:46:09,563
[SPEAKER_00]: You know what the crop is,

513
00:46:10,682 --> 00:46:14,825
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't know, well, you learn what a crop is, you learn where the outlines of it are.

514
00:46:18,268 --> 00:46:34,040
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot not be aware of the choice, as I said, and that's a good thing, because if the problem were truly the fact that animal can still have a desire or real will, well, arahantyu would never be possible, because you have no say in that.

515
00:46:35,576 --> 00:46:39,839
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, that's what contemporarily, generally, people would think.

516
00:46:39,979 --> 00:46:44,882
[SPEAKER_00]: If I try hard enough, determine hard enough, concentrate hard enough, these things will just disappear.

517
00:46:45,943 --> 00:46:47,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were always the problem.

518
00:46:47,524 --> 00:46:48,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

519
00:46:50,446 --> 00:46:55,149
[SPEAKER_00]: The animal pressuring you to go, well, the animal trying to go to the crop,

520
00:46:58,618 --> 00:46:59,758
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just what animal does.

521
00:47:00,118 --> 00:47:07,380
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognizing that and tolerating it, that's the doorway, that's the problem, that's the crossroad, that's a mistake.

522
00:47:08,240 --> 00:47:09,300
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the obstruction.

523
00:47:10,481 --> 00:47:12,041
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why arahant should be as possible.

524
00:47:13,501 --> 00:47:20,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stop doing it, you stop fueling it, you stop reinforcing it through the, as I said, ignorant choice, animal will calm down.

525
00:47:22,071 --> 00:47:24,052
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, countless suttas describe that.

526
00:47:24,592 --> 00:47:27,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Through training in virtue, you develop samādhi.

527
00:47:27,053 --> 00:47:28,934
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop internal, actual peace.

528
00:47:29,555 --> 00:47:30,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Effortless peace.

529
00:47:31,035 --> 00:47:37,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Not peace dependent on the circumstances and right retreat conditions or right inspiration or something.

530
00:47:37,659 --> 00:47:37,799
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

531
00:47:38,099 --> 00:47:41,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Peace on account of the animal not wanting those crops anymore.

532
00:47:42,341 --> 00:47:45,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Genuine safety.

533
00:47:45,822 --> 00:47:48,804
[SPEAKER_00]: The simile of the, you know, the relay chariots as well.

534
00:47:49,664 --> 00:47:50,885
[SPEAKER_00]: That's always what comes to mind.

535
00:47:52,850 --> 00:47:56,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, it will... a purification of virtue, i.e.

536
00:47:56,873 --> 00:48:06,000
[SPEAKER_00]: training the oxen, leads to the purification of the mind, leads to samādhi, which then leads to the purification of the views.

537
00:48:08,041 --> 00:48:11,724
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what I've said, like the questioning now, why does appropriation happen?

538
00:48:11,764 --> 00:48:17,528
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like questioning the views while the crop, you know, is still not guarded.

539
00:48:19,142 --> 00:48:25,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you guard the crop, animal actually calms down, you see through that, then you will understand appropriation.

540
00:48:25,785 --> 00:48:28,045
[SPEAKER_00]: The views will be purified on account of that.

541
00:48:29,666 --> 00:48:31,027
[SPEAKER_00]: And same works with the right view.

542
00:48:32,387 --> 00:48:36,009
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a... it's not a... it's not a magic bullet that just hits you out of nowhere.

543
00:48:38,169 --> 00:48:40,890
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, you start taming, as the Sutta says.

544
00:48:41,291 --> 00:48:43,752
[SPEAKER_00]: A Sutta Upatīcchana does this because he's a Suttava.

545
00:48:44,572 --> 00:48:47,313
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody is Suttava and learns, he'll stop doing.

546
00:48:48,285 --> 00:48:52,349
[SPEAKER_00]: He stops allowing the oxen to go into sensuality, to act out of ill will.

547
00:48:52,429 --> 00:48:54,111
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll start training it, because he learned.

548
00:48:55,373 --> 00:48:58,216
[SPEAKER_00]: He starts training it, because he learned the oxen will come down.

549
00:48:59,254 --> 00:49:09,400
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll get to see it and then he get to recognize that this sense of I am is dependent on the existing components of this simultaneously existing experience as a whole, or aggregates, whatever you want to describe it.

550
00:49:10,040 --> 00:49:16,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And he will be unable to now hold on to it as the center of experience, as mine, or find safety in it.

551
00:49:17,265 --> 00:49:18,665
[SPEAKER_00]: He will be free from ātavāda.

552
00:49:19,686 --> 00:49:22,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Follows the same principle, he'll be free from the sense of I am.

553
00:49:24,389 --> 00:49:24,729
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

554
00:49:28,498 --> 00:49:34,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, those connections, those perceptions, need to be made through diligent effort by you.

555
00:49:35,120 --> 00:49:48,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... Well, probably not any of you, but I think generally, like a wider audience, you can't really blame people for it, but, like, when you hear this, I think many people would just then, aha, okay, so I'll just keep the precepts.

556
00:49:49,763 --> 00:49:54,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, obviously, listen, I listened to this talk for two hours, and clearly precepts are very important.

557
00:49:55,552 --> 00:49:56,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll just keep the precepts.

558
00:49:57,413 --> 00:50:03,636
[SPEAKER_00]: With implicit belief that now everything will fall into place, because I adopted the new set of external observances.

559
00:50:04,936 --> 00:50:15,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But internally, a person remains still not making connections, not drawing right perceptions, not drawing right associations, not showing right things to the animal when it pressures you.

560
00:50:15,862 --> 00:50:17,582
[SPEAKER_00]: And just, okay, no, no, no.

561
00:50:17,722 --> 00:50:20,684
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, you do have a basis.

562
00:50:21,404 --> 00:50:25,046
[SPEAKER_00]: You did get the first chariot of virtue, but you're not really moving it.

563
00:50:26,430 --> 00:50:29,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not getting any closer to the actual samādhi, because you're doing it blindly.

564
00:50:31,194 --> 00:50:39,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to learn about the virtue, about the precepts, so that you can understand the principles behind that training.

565
00:50:39,079 --> 00:50:43,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And the principles behind that training is, obviously, what we spoke so many times in so many other talks.

566
00:50:44,563 --> 00:50:49,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Watching for your intentions, not just, well, the rule says this, I don't do it, and I don't think about it.

567
00:50:51,717 --> 00:50:53,118
[SPEAKER_00]: But what if there is no rule?

568
00:50:53,178 --> 00:50:55,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there were no rules in the beginning.

569
00:50:56,080 --> 00:50:56,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

570
00:50:57,020 --> 00:50:57,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine that.

571
00:50:59,141 --> 00:51:04,084
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha just told the dhamma and everybody who heard it, who understood it, knew what to do.

572
00:51:06,565 --> 00:51:09,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean killing was allowed?

573
00:51:10,127 --> 00:51:12,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Sexual intercourse was allowed?

574
00:51:12,909 --> 00:51:13,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Lying was allowed?

575
00:51:15,150 --> 00:51:17,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it wasn't stated that it wasn't allowed.

576
00:51:18,872 --> 00:51:19,593
[SPEAKER_00]: How did they know?

577
00:51:21,517 --> 00:51:29,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, do I know why those things that, well, later were defined as not allowed?

578
00:51:31,022 --> 00:51:33,964
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, do I know why those things are an obstacle?

579
00:51:33,984 --> 00:51:40,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Or am I just keeping the precepts because, well, I was told to do so, and I have faith in that, I find it inspiring.

580
00:51:40,167 --> 00:51:41,208
[SPEAKER_00]: But, no, no, no.

581
00:51:41,348 --> 00:51:44,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I know for myself the principle?

582
00:51:46,234 --> 00:51:50,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Why sexuality is an obstruction to seeing the dhamma, fulfilling the dhamma?

583
00:51:51,136 --> 00:51:55,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Why acting out of ill will is an obstruction to seeing the dhamma, to fulfilling the dhamma?

584
00:51:58,339 --> 00:52:09,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Why, as I said, the wrong speech, gossip, lying, comfort, beautification, why are those things actual obstacle towards fulfilling the dhamma?

585
00:52:12,270 --> 00:52:15,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't know, well, that's your priority, really.

586
00:52:15,631 --> 00:52:19,293
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why your virtue is not leading to samādhi, which is not leading to the right view.

587
00:52:20,813 --> 00:52:22,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you stopped the chariot.

588
00:52:22,414 --> 00:52:23,754
[SPEAKER_00]: You took it for granted.

589
00:52:23,834 --> 00:52:26,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You thought getting the chariot of virtue is all I need.

590
00:52:26,395 --> 00:52:26,995
[SPEAKER_00]: I got the car.

591
00:52:27,516 --> 00:52:28,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It's right there, in my driveway.

592
00:52:30,977 --> 00:52:33,518
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not going with it in the right direction, basically.

593
00:52:37,672 --> 00:52:41,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhante, you often say that while appropriation goes away, the sense of self remains.

594
00:52:41,394 --> 00:52:42,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just mine anymore.

595
00:52:42,755 --> 00:52:50,199
[SPEAKER_00]: If I, my, and I am all go away, what, in the Sutta terms, is the thing remaining that feels like the sense of self?

596
00:52:50,559 --> 00:53:00,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, see, the problem with this question is that it assumes Puthujjana's point of view and Arahant's point of view at the same time and expects an answer that can satisfy both.

597
00:53:04,103 --> 00:53:09,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And, if I give you an answer that can satisfy both, that won't be the dhamma, because it's not opanaiko.

598
00:53:09,829 --> 00:53:11,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't be leading onwards towards the practice.

599
00:53:11,691 --> 00:53:23,482
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be an irrelevant theoretical information that might be correct on the paper, but it completely kills the practice, because it led you to believe that now you know what that is, while you don't.

600
00:53:24,443 --> 00:53:28,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Putujana cannot know what an arahant is, unless he's on the way of becoming one.

601
00:53:30,627 --> 00:53:34,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what remains is what you will see if you follow what the Sutta says.

602
00:53:39,095 --> 00:53:47,062
[SPEAKER_00]: When I speak about the sense of self remains, I speak about it practically for those that still have sense of self, because usually that is my audience.

603
00:53:47,983 --> 00:53:49,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Those that don't, they don't need to listen to me.

604
00:53:51,186 --> 00:53:54,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's more beneficial, practically, to not fall into the view

605
00:53:55,993 --> 00:53:57,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, the modern science has proven nothing.

606
00:53:57,934 --> 00:53:58,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is constantly changing.

607
00:53:58,935 --> 00:54:00,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, everything is anatta.

608
00:54:00,116 --> 00:54:01,396
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha was right and so on.

609
00:54:01,417 --> 00:54:02,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Myself is not real.

610
00:54:03,498 --> 00:54:03,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It is real.

611
00:54:04,739 --> 00:54:07,040
[SPEAKER_00]: The rainbow is real.

612
00:54:07,060 --> 00:54:08,381
[SPEAKER_00]: The sound of the lute is real.

613
00:54:10,782 --> 00:54:14,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you say it's not mine, do you regard it as not real?

614
00:54:15,425 --> 00:54:17,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you regard it as real, just not mine?

615
00:54:18,367 --> 00:54:20,048
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a huge difference there.

616
00:54:21,970 --> 00:54:24,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Because more often than not, it's like, oh, not mine, not real.

617
00:54:25,072 --> 00:54:25,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Dismissed.

618
00:54:26,987 --> 00:54:30,371
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you're dismissing it, basically, you're ignoring the problem.

619
00:54:31,211 --> 00:54:32,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I say, no, it is yours.

620
00:54:34,194 --> 00:54:38,999
[SPEAKER_00]: First, acknowledge that it is yours, so that you can see the thing that is yours.

621
00:54:39,300 --> 00:54:40,281
[SPEAKER_00]: See it for what it is.

622
00:54:40,421 --> 00:54:43,584
[SPEAKER_00]: The ambiguous rainbow that's not material.

623
00:54:43,624 --> 00:54:46,167
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't find it in anything more palpable.

624
00:54:47,138 --> 00:54:49,780
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that tendency to find more palpable things.

625
00:54:50,721 --> 00:54:56,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty much why every puthujjana's effort will fall onto one wrong view or the other.

626
00:54:57,446 --> 00:55:08,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learning to see the rainbow, learning to hear the sound of the lute correctly, means undoing that gratuitous dependence on palpability being the measure of realness.

627
00:55:09,355 --> 00:55:13,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for as long as that is the state of one's mind, your own feelings won't be regarded as real.

628
00:55:14,999 --> 00:55:16,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own thoughts won't be regarded as real.

629
00:55:19,265 --> 00:55:19,846
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is real.

630
00:55:21,247 --> 00:55:21,648
[SPEAKER_00]: It's yours.

631
00:55:22,489 --> 00:55:22,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Take it up.

632
00:55:24,371 --> 00:55:28,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, hold on, this, which is mine, does not quite obey my will.

633
00:55:30,037 --> 00:55:31,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so, it is real.

634
00:55:32,340 --> 00:55:34,543
[SPEAKER_00]: It just showed me that it has its own inertia.

635
00:55:35,684 --> 00:55:38,988
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on things that are completely not in my control, equally real.

636
00:55:41,437 --> 00:55:43,439
[SPEAKER_00]: I can say it's mine, but is it really mine?

637
00:55:44,099 --> 00:55:45,821
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes obvious to you that it's not mine.

638
00:55:46,221 --> 00:55:48,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That it cannot be yours, even if you want it to.

639
00:55:49,604 --> 00:55:50,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all.

640
00:55:50,405 --> 00:55:50,965
[SPEAKER_00]: That's anatta.

641
00:55:55,269 --> 00:55:57,290
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to go further than that.

642
00:55:57,671 --> 00:56:00,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you need to sustain this, protect it and develop it in that sense.

643
00:56:00,733 --> 00:56:05,137
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't need more answers and permutations and so on.

644
00:56:05,918 --> 00:56:06,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Because

645
00:56:07,821 --> 00:56:11,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of self, whatever it is, well, first, for most people, it's not even seen.

646
00:56:12,164 --> 00:56:13,504
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see it real as such.

647
00:56:13,765 --> 00:56:15,365
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see the rainbow real as such.

648
00:56:16,466 --> 00:56:19,668
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see the sound of the lute real as such, neither here nor there.

649
00:56:20,288 --> 00:56:21,509
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of the point, isn't it?

650
00:56:22,270 --> 00:56:26,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, wherever you look for the sound of the lute, it won't be there.

651
00:56:26,512 --> 00:56:27,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's there.

652
00:56:27,893 --> 00:56:33,296
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there when all these there's are there, but it's not in any individual one of them.

653
00:56:33,756 --> 00:56:34,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with the rainbow.

654
00:56:35,577 --> 00:56:36,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It's neither here nor there.

655
00:56:38,770 --> 00:56:44,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of I am, sense of self, even your own atavāda, as coarse as it can be, it's neither here nor there.

656
00:56:45,174 --> 00:56:46,575
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is real as such.

657
00:56:47,035 --> 00:56:54,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So stop seeking it on the level of palpability and recognize it real as such as that which is neither here nor there.

658
00:56:56,121 --> 00:56:56,581
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I know?

659
00:56:56,621 --> 00:56:57,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, ask yourself a question.

660
00:56:57,662 --> 00:56:59,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have a sense of I am?

661
00:56:59,483 --> 00:57:01,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have a sense of whatever, whoever, whoever I am?

662
00:57:02,525 --> 00:57:03,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Say your name.

663
00:57:03,085 --> 00:57:04,066
[SPEAKER_00]: You do have a sense.

664
00:57:05,046 --> 00:57:07,688
[SPEAKER_00]: When you look for where that sense is, you're not gonna find it.

665
00:57:09,047 --> 00:57:17,913
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, Asutava Putujana's default kind of direction that he will fall into is, ah, therefore it's not real.

666
00:57:18,533 --> 00:57:24,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind does... well, he himself does not see any... any phenomenological nuances in between.

667
00:57:26,338 --> 00:57:28,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I can't see it, smell it, taste it, touch it, it's not real.

668
00:57:31,501 --> 00:57:34,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, people would live their whole life like that, thinking their own thoughts are not really real.

669
00:57:35,494 --> 00:57:39,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Their own intentions that make them smell, taste, hear and touch are not real.

670
00:57:42,856 --> 00:57:45,676
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like what you need to... what needs to be recognized.

671
00:57:45,696 --> 00:57:52,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's what I mean, like the question is kind of assumes the validity of the position that pertains to the beginning and the end of the internal path.

672
00:57:53,218 --> 00:57:55,918
[SPEAKER_00]: And providing an answer to that will not be the dhamma.

673
00:57:57,318 --> 00:58:00,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you satisfy both seemingly, but in reality you can't do that.

674
00:58:01,539 --> 00:58:04,660
[SPEAKER_00]: You are either on one side or you're trailing towards the other side.

675
00:58:06,578 --> 00:58:07,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is left there?

676
00:58:07,919 --> 00:58:10,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, find out by traveling there yourself.

677
00:58:12,262 --> 00:58:15,845
[SPEAKER_00]: And you travel there yourself by not denying what you have now.

678
00:58:17,126 --> 00:58:26,712
[SPEAKER_00]: By learning how not to own it through undermining its ownership, well, through undermining your ownership of it, and certainly not through denying it or dismissing it.

679
00:58:29,815 --> 00:58:34,238
[SPEAKER_00]: You often emphasize not practicing a subha as a way to get rid of the pressure.

680
00:58:36,132 --> 00:58:42,193
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of the simile, could we say it's important to pin down the ox, endure the pressure before the beating perception of a subha?

681
00:58:42,813 --> 00:58:47,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Before... yes, well... well, depends how you... what you mean.

682
00:58:47,334 --> 00:58:55,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if in your example, pinning the ox is enduring the pressure, and beating is the perception of a subha, then, yeah, sure.

683
00:58:56,476 --> 00:59:01,918
[SPEAKER_00]: But for somebody, pinning down the ox might be the subha, and beating it is the enduring.

684
00:59:02,438 --> 00:59:02,818
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

685
00:59:04,518 --> 00:59:14,961
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, yeah, like, if, say, you see the clouds coming up from the horizon, before the pressure is fully developed, then, yeah, you should definitely do a subha.

686
00:59:16,041 --> 00:59:17,402
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the subha is done.

687
00:59:19,062 --> 00:59:21,563
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where it can pertain to the mind rightly.

688
00:59:22,483 --> 00:59:28,365
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are now amidst of trying to hold back the bull,

689
00:59:29,430 --> 00:59:35,953
[SPEAKER_00]: And failing, the bull is too strong, and you want the whole thing just to end, and you're doing a subha.

690
00:59:36,473 --> 00:59:37,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the general subha.

691
00:59:37,834 --> 00:59:39,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how people do it.

692
00:59:39,435 --> 00:59:40,315
[SPEAKER_00]: State of mind came up.

693
00:59:40,475 --> 00:59:44,617
[SPEAKER_00]: I must get rid of it, because I cannot contain it.

694
00:59:44,657 --> 00:59:45,858
[SPEAKER_00]: And why I cannot contain it?

695
00:59:45,878 --> 00:59:46,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I already said that.

696
00:59:47,158 --> 00:59:49,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I haven't looked at the horizon at all.

697
00:59:49,279 --> 00:59:50,560
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't paid attention to it.

698
00:59:51,380 --> 00:59:55,582
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't discerned its inclinations, preferences growing.

